---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Point-in-Time"
  description="What was actually knowable on a given date, and what existed but had not been published yet."
  eyebrow="No-lookahead guarantee"
  heading="Point-in-time demonstration"
  intro="Pick a date. The left column is what a model running that morning could legitimately read. The right column is everything else — real figures, already describing the past, that nobody could see yet."
>
  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="cutoff-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="cutoff-h">Information cutoff</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/pit?as_of=</span>
    </div>
    <div class="controls">
      <label class="field" for="pit-date">
        As of date
        <input type="date" id="pit-date" name="pit-date" value="2025-03-01" />
      </label>
      <p class="prose" style="margin:0">
        The snapshot is rebuilt server-side for whatever date you choose, from the same
        <code>release_date</code> key the compute plane joins on.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div id="pit-summary" style="margin-top:1rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="why-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="why-h">Why this page exists</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">FINDYN_V1_SPEC.md §14.1</span>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every macro figure carries <strong>two</strong> dates. One is the period it describes —
        January&rsquo;s inflation, the fourth quarter&rsquo;s profits. The other is the date it
        became <strong>knowable</strong>: the morning the statistical agency actually published it,
        typically two to eight weeks later, and later still for anything revised.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most datasets store only the first. Join on that alone and a backtest quietly reads
        January&rsquo;s inflation print on the 31st of January — a number that did not exist for
        another two weeks. Nothing errors, no test fails, and the model looks prescient because it
        has been handed tomorrow&rsquo;s newspaper. Every result downstream is fiction, and the
        distortion is largest exactly where it matters most: around turning points, when revisions
        are heaviest and the lag is longest.
      </p>
      <p>
        FinDyn stores <code>obs_date</code> and <code>release_date</code> separately, and one
        function — <code>pit_join</code> — is the only sanctioned way to read macro history in the
        compute plane. This page runs that same rule in SQL and shows you both halves of the split:
        what survived the cutoff, and what was correctly held back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pit-cols">
    <section class="pit-col pit-col--known" aria-labelledby="known-h">
      <h2 id="known-h">Known on this date</h2>
      <p class="pit-col__count" id="pit-known-count">—</p>
      <p class="pit-col__caption">
        Released on or before the cutoff. This is the entire information set a causal model is
        allowed to use.
      </p>
      <div id="pit-known"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="pit-col pit-col--withheld" aria-labelledby="withheld-h">
      <h2 id="withheld-h">Existed but NOT yet published</h2>
      <p class="pit-col__count" id="pit-withheld-count">—</p>
      <p class="pit-col__caption">
        In the database today, describing the past — and invisible on the cutoff date. Every row
        here is a lookahead bug that a period-only join would have introduced silently.
      </p>
      <div id="pit-withheld"></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/pit';
</script>
